#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125581} :activity/id #uuid "dd6d8fc7-ed4f-5412-9ac7-72ddd6e6fc3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164 :topic/id #uuid "84c65e66-54ce-5cc4-9970-19a6aba25468" :topic/math-academy-id 1888} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124372 :question/id #uuid "c844db9d-a556-5ce4-90e8-2db1b5671579" :question/math-academy-id "e-6684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522755}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "4f190feb-818e-5e2a-8ed1-9a841eb19c11" :step/math-academy-id 24350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125713 :tutorial/content "When we deal with functions, we are often interested in their largest or smallest values.\n\n- The **global maximum** of a function is the largest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum. It's also called the **absolute maximum.**\n\n- The **global minimum** of a function is the smallest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum. It's also called the **absolute minimum.**\n\nIf one or both exist, the global maximum and minimum values of a function are collectively called the **global extrema** of the function. \n\nFor example, in the graph below, the global maximum is $2$, while the global minimum is $-2$.\n\n![](images/22/2235cefbb995edcc085f96d8a5ff4e1ddd83f6345091c90ec3e0a7797fb9d8f3.png)\n\nThe point where a function reaches its global maximum/minimum is called the **global maximum/minimum point**.  So in this case, \n\n- $(-2,2)$ is the global maximum point, and\n\n- $(1,-2)$ is the global minimum point.\n\n**Watch out!** The *global maximum/minimum* is unique. It's a value of the function ($y$-coordinate on the graph). However, there can be several *global maximum/minimum points*. All of these will have the same $y$-coordinate (*global maximum/minimum*) but different $x$-coordinates." :tutorial/id #uuid "cfedbc54-8dd6-532f-9075-109e336b43a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1495 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a61d8ec6-2820-5303-a832-f51a73657cc2" :step/math-academy-id 3625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124206 :question/id #uuid "30557b46-f1f0-51dc-94ed-5d84515bc4b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507446}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "ce76583f-0ff9-522f-8324-5c6a6492916a" :step/math-academy-id 3626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124207 :question/id #uuid "c7c94a49-1a4e-51f6-b0e7-213c67f40fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1113"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist"} :step/id #uuid "a936946d-a2c6-5c5b-8e3e-ad8903a06ff2" :step/math-academy-id 3629}] :activity/title "Global Extrema of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124693}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069578} :activity/id #uuid "09fac72c-e8cf-5881-9f87-6135d15c419b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652 :topic/id #uuid "b27952f8-4bda-51a9-b51b-57fe61de4a72" :topic/math-academy-id 470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070022 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule that takes a number as **input** and then **outputs** another number.\n\nFor instance, let's look at the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x) = 3x+1\n$$\n\nIn this example,\n\n- $f$ is the **name** of the function,\n\n- the number $x$ is the **input**,\n\n- the number $3x+1$ is the **output**.\n\nIf we **substitute** a number into our function in place of $x$, we get another number as output. \n\nFor example, by substituting $x={\\color{blue}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{2}}) = 3\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}+1 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{blue}{2}}$ results in an output of $7.$\n\nWe can substitute any number we like into this function! For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{-2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{-2}}) = 3\\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}})+1 = -5.\n$$\n\nSo, the input $x={\\color{red}{-2}}$ results in an output of $-5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "902a3802-7423-5fa0-9cf1-43856055c65c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8783 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "726a2782-726c-5f7e-b8e5-138b8563ebff" :step/math-academy-id 138156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068486 :question/id #uuid "38228952-21ef-5caf-9805-76e42ebf0caa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522723}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8af30817-9be7-537a-9bfb-2ce31b525422" :step/math-academy-id 138157 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070023 :tutorial/content "Functions with more than one input are called **multivariable functions**. \n\nFor example, the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x-y\n$$\n\ntakes in two numbers, $x$ and $y$, and returns their difference. \n\nFor multivariable functions, the order of the inputs matters. For example, if we substitute $x={\\color{blue}{3}}$ and $y={\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}{3}},{\\color{red}{2}}) = {\\color{blue}{3}}-{\\color{red}{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nwhereas if we substitute $x={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{3}},$ we get a different output:\n\n$$\nf({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{3}})={\\color{red}{2}}-{\\color{blue}{3}}=-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bef6d6f-bda2-56dd-badb-0001ca2e4329" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8784 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Functions of More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "004a3461-1ea4-5e28-807b-fea6c51ae3d4" :step/math-academy-id 138161 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068487 :question/id #uuid "4e06fa0b-f3f0-5e60-9a83-67a393b4cc07" :question/math-academy-id "e-14150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b5f42de-fad6-54b6-90e1-205fdbe8a456" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492852}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "e7679093-39bc-5822-a013-8ae88e7f542e" :step/math-academy-id 138162}] :activity/title "Introduction to Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068995}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492946} :activity/id #uuid "32d73cc0-19b4-59d2-9583-c970a787e5ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652 :topic/id #uuid "b27952f8-4bda-51a9-b51b-57fe61de4a72" :topic/math-academy-id 470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1864d1-334e-5ed1-a854-e574404fd88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492368} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e76a0e-856c-5852-affb-986c061df288" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "135b40fb-5cf8-57bf-b723-676d052a026d" :question/math-academy-id "q-214832" :question/problem "If $f(x)={x}^{2}-{4}^{x-2},$ then $f(4)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=4$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-{4}^{x-2} \\\\ f(4) & ={4}^{2}-{4}^{4-2} \\\\  & =16-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =16-16 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "235c73d5-ae23-5b39-841a-4951560a5eff"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019dfabe-776e-5411-a52a-468bd8010e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=-29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492226} {:answer/id #uuid "1d371280-0e81-5a2f-8015-37b237435f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492274} {:answer/id #uuid "28841ece-193a-5984-aca8-980d7d142fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492301} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf18704-fb98-57f4-be11-81048d8cd298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492495} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6261f9-d215-5ae3-bfdb-9431ed756997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-3)=-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492496} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bd600f-4df3-5837-8db3-4c610cb01b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e0ff0f4-e55f-55b3-97b5-30132033682d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12993" :question/problem "Evaluate $g(-3)$, where $g(x)=(x+1)^{2}-(x-2)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-3$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =(x+1)^{2}-(x-2)^{2} \\\\ g(-3) & =(-3+1)^{2}-(-3-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =4-25 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45066582-f649-5b97-b9fa-5d0d0116ad1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea9fe05-136c-54fa-8287-468235cbdb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492316} {:answer/id #uuid "92ff2ec6-c1ed-57da-a105-85732944baad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492504} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dd854f-76b8-57b7-925b-6bec0085d762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492611} {:answer/id #uuid "c81618e6-76ad-5708-8e7c-de8c549d1cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492616} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb62b1a-34c6-56d6-983c-37f403386609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492616} :answer-field/id #uuid "021d4422-18e9-573a-aea3-c035cc557e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e8b84ea-82b6-5138-bf4d-e73af61fdac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130576" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(-1,2)$ if $f(x,y)=(2x-y)(x+2y).$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =(2x-y)(x+2y) \\\\ f(-1,2) & =(2(-1)-2)((-1)+2(2)) \\\\  & =(-4)(3) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "719d3968-3672-57cb-89f5-452d75b3654a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04609b43-5111-5208-b18d-1c6e7b99af15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492230} {:answer/id #uuid "16f29b86-ae62-561b-a969-655e99943a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492260} {:answer/id #uuid "28262c89-1983-5b3b-9aa2-400cb80daebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492299} {:answer/id #uuid "8319ede1-808f-592a-a403-f22857a5c9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492481} {:answer/id #uuid "b5da9e45-5970-5fa3-92b6-9e353c5519c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "g(-1)=-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492230} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da22270-2bc9-5e75-a1ee-38a321ba33ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9348d7e-52bc-5a07-98ca-88ef4ff5b0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31727" :question/problem "Evaluate $g(-1),$ if $g(x)=(x+3)^{2}-(x-5)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-1$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =(x+3)^{2}-(x-5)^{2} \\\\ g(-1) & =((-1)+3)^{2}-((-1)-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-(-6)^{2} \\\\  & =4-36 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "75570071-e9a9-567a-93e7-3ab7178838f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492957}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492206}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928} :activity/id #uuid "4c188df7-e524-54c2-8a5d-b2f16e8dfb77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472 :topic/id #uuid "f7e72507-2c32-5978-b2b5-175e533dfdf0" :topic/math-academy-id 2022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130482 :tutorial/content "The **roots** of a function are the values of the input $x$ for which the function equals zero. For this reason, roots are sometimes also called **zeros**.\n\nAlgebraically, the roots of a function are the solutions for $x$ in the following equation:\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\nFor example, if $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1,$ then we can find the roots by solving the equation $f(x) = 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this function has a single root: $x=-2.$\n\nGraphically, the roots of a function are the $x$-intercepts. For example, for the graph of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x+1$ shown below, we can see that the function intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/a7/a75e8a0ae40c5cc940f9fe2d9194b0dcb5c6db2742e87a6d903dc15c0b970e99.png)\n\nWhen shown on a graph, the roots of a function are also called the $\\boldsymbol x$**-intercepts.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "37a5be36-f2b1-5ebb-91b6-98ccbc9babaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8802 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "14413a03-0cc3-58a8-9a8d-a19f98f83fa5" :step/math-academy-id 146046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128873 :question/id #uuid "c1e36290-8271-5508-9bf8-cf15a3907f75" :question/math-academy-id "e-14174"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f5682f-73b8-5c0a-b7d3-3336200843a0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503696}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b4ec4b62-fcff-5a84-8aff-d4e6e77464a0" :step/math-academy-id 146047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128874 :question/id #uuid "a443151c-f310-533e-adb7-1c8eda168738" :question/math-academy-id "e-14176"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02e29602-b3c0-5ceb-ab65-710c