<y<3,$ which can also be written as $-1<f(x)<3.$"} :step/id #uuid "aba196a5-a956-50e2-b8a6-61106b7dc46b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151cd673-35b1-5901-b1e4-3f2f71811a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488517}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488517} :answer-field/id #uuid "af246519-227d-5a39-92f0-c9fb3022be21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c165e391-96c0-5f1a-8559-109c7f748bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488861} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4799eb0-92e5-5e8a-a05b-b1fc23cc5f4d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489116} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ae91eb-e7d1-5b05-ab82-7c77711c0e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488641} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbd8104-f926-5b39-b3f0-4089b321c8ec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2636e2eb-9d96-5c30-898f-0b55c9dd4bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-236386" :question/problem "![](images/20/20cf8d018f748534720eb3efe21c4adf9354a015d824dcc15bfd9c33ad14a895.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note  that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $\\infty ,$ but not including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1<y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$1<f(x)<\\infty .$"} :step/id #uuid "ceb88876-37e1-5b22-af2a-9cf0a535596a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489363}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489363 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e1d5456-f9d4-5149-86a2-9ba9a7c28a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488725} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b650656-bd36-5828-a479-5f52c0e48b4b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2520b781-4fa8-5f19-abe9-c773ef419534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488546} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b7ed819-da82-5a1b-a80b-815d82f58698" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273427b6-1181-5cc4-9482-efd6c25dd700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488554} :answer-field/id #uuid "7167fd1b-94c1-5af4-adca-6b79f3ab33a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92babace-3fcf-5d4a-8789-c7e1f3a12026" :question/math-academy-id "q-236716" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a837faad775bb111760b2e90920c0490c42473c77f74f8be7a46271df154d97.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (-3,1)$ is $(-1,3)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [1,4)$ is $\\{1\\}$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-1,3)\\cup \\{1\\}=(-1,3),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-1<f(x)<3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the values $y=-1$ and $y=3$ are NOT valid outputs of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use \"less than\" $(<)$ instead of \"less than or equal to\" $(\\le )$ at these endpoints."} :step/id #uuid "cf6a1dfd-5754-5ce6-84a6-e24b8966c20d"}] :activity/title "The Range of a Function: Advanced Cases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488449}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490353} :activity/id #uuid "a4407aef-3121-52a9-b946-f3e8ebff0a98" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580 :topic/id #uuid "557f8b58-be49-555e-8162-3d1448eaff0c" :topic/math-academy-id 3728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077fd3b6-a66f-5992-89ff-f734285036e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1<f(x)<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489533} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd4f26c-68d7-508b-ae68-df55769b27e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty <f(x)<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489797} {:answer/id #uuid "85a2736a-d41e-5e05-9a88-0d47451e0a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489820} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ab8a0a-f5d1-5b79-8ad9-de72a82f973b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty <f(x)\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489884} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f2f3c-997e-5379-8c75-90a1c8d674a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489797} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7dd7694-ccde-505f-9224-5e793348c4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24be045a-a1e0-562b-a001-f17a0f6f12b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79997" :question/problem "![](images/ac/acbf4ae592e7912e6e27415ce99f872c4c0ec1737cfa3269635f37f7fa0fe5db.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- There is no smallest $y$-value.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-\\infty $ and $1,$ but not including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-\\infty <y<1,$ which can also be written as  $-\\infty <f(x)<1.$"} :step/id #uuid "027ab44b-48d6-5961-8e71-f93826f26196" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e2fe1e-5ce8-5259-883a-e27ba55b0ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489908} :answer-field/id #uuid "2356dd8e-95af-53aa-99c1-588dd0a4fe73" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a84cca-e35f-5c4a-b07e-845a7d28433f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489667} :answer-field/id #uuid "3443567b-7d91-5f03-9d4e-6b2b217a9066" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e903e5-f2b3-5ed4-a09b-70f4dda6a048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489807} :answer-field/id #uuid "9abeb5f8-371d-515e-a1f6-7daf65af6f1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c426db9-03a0-51ed-9522-bdc02ff647af" :question/math-academy-id "q-236388" :question/problem "![](images/49/49288e2c082854cfc27a2d48af2d82a96016a3c1394066471d49873e4c75878e.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x=0.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $\\infty ,$ including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$1\\le f(x)<\\infty .$"} :step/id #uuid "356e531e-4a8b-5a5a-96af-77c558013231" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39cc2ab9-0e69-5e63-9804-2b53b2eb63cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-2,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489645} {:answer/id #uuid "5825da8a-a8cd-5ffe-bc88-0e82b360e91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489725} {:answer/id #uuid "698e15a5-80be-55ee-8a03-6ab33045d444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489769} {:answer/id #uuid "b168c6fa-3346-5972-afe9-b5a59f8ae3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-2,3)\\cup (3,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489903} {:answer/id #uuid "b8291b42-df3a-58f9-a0f8-316a3f99fa6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489920}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489645} :answer-field/id #uuid "61208644-8c5b-5f4d-81d6-4e752cb4edbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d214df3-aafe-5a52-bd67-2c23fbad240f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34734" :question/problem "![](images/58/582236fbb64c0ebeda310cb0007435d4044295b232499ff5771ede846a1b6d05.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-4,-1)$ is $[0,4]$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-1,1)$ is $[-2,2)$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[0,4]\\cup [-2,2)=[-2,4].$"} :step/id #uuid "537a7146-75a4-56c9-a7db-1e94a31e5c00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b528277-bd1d-5076-b163-8f585cd1adce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cap " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489649} {:answer/id #uuid "52b3e7b8-7456-581e-bfd1-f45bbe1051d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cup " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489710} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1a5b0b-3cf6-5be3-bc9c-ae896e933954" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2e451d-3033-5f0d-89f1-815a64077872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489537} {:answer/id #uuid "3294b297-4b44-5f75-9b72-739bc720849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489628} {:answer/id #uuid "da0ff875-c917-5972-a3c4-675de3efc1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489995} {:answer/id #uuid "f543cbe4-e2c6-5a46-b0cd-da91aa24619c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490046} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1dd13d9-fa2a-56fa-b820-f5ccac336683" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cf3e4b-7a20-5728-8bde-7ff22aeef87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489530} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd8a5f6-91fd-5671-a481-b81be219a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489551} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1d3a76-5cfb-54b2-8951-80ac1e030c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489647} {:answer/id #uuid "7870d536-7778-52d6-b0f4-199737c42072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489530} :answer-field/id #uuid "d188c86d-e40c-5825-8f05-79c9182cde6b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5d6b2e7-1b54-5bc0-b6ea-58d113518a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-236465" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fae9ac16ebabea7054903ab75f2305f98c757f8ee7fc4924600774e627ad76b4.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n$\\,$  The range is $f(x)\\in $\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (-3,0]$ is $[-2,0)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (0,3)$ is $(1,3]$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,0)\\cup (1,3].$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the values $y=0$ and $y=1$ are NOT valid outputs of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use parentheses $(…)$ instead of brackets $[…]$ at these endpoints."} :step/id #uuid "b4b9a850-1b05-55cc-93b4-fc159fb75d80"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d05cb0d-8d25-5449-ac1c-8b614908715e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489543} {:answer/id #uuid "1b96b9f5-5dee-542b-9536-5184c253d217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489577} {:answer/id #uuid "536d01f7-fbaf-515c-9cab-bbacb8844c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<f(x)\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489711} {:answer/id #uuid "61686cce-e8eb-5e02-8245-808eec7d2bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489744} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d3bfaa-3cdb-5dfc-8e12-4bf0044664fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490050} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c513d9a-67e4-587c-bfd8-0a60d03dc406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30dbc182-568e-5645-9ba2-4447169ef008" :question/math-academy-id "q-129372" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb292b77908dbb4590b6bbc49c452215edd8e6668e003e0984074a7ca22bf4ff.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs when $x=2.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $\\infty ,$ including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as  $0\\le f(x)<\\infty .$"} :step/id #uuid "bff636d5-d253-5f8b-b379-4b8793e7f844" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490348}}] :activity/title "The Range of a Function: Advanced Cases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489508}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130268} :activity/id #uuid "4cbf00b2-eac9-5ab3-8d90-7968e6bac7c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050633 :topic/id #uuid "5a8a9cda-b760-543a-ae76-9ef9c11f9aa4" :topic/math-academy-id 1985} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130334 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the functions $g$ and $f$ with the mapping diagrams shown below. What will the value $f(g(2))$ be?\n\n![](images/a3/a36715b7fb042dcb568dfc4be0520b1856a49bec3259c224594278188fd6878a.png)\n\nThe notation $f(g(2))$ means that we first find the value of $g(2)$ and then put the result into the function $f$.\nFrom the left diagram above, $2$ goes to $3$, so $g(2)=3$. Now, the diagram on the right tells us that $3$ goes to $-5$. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\nf(g(2)) = f(3) =-5\n$$\n\n![](images/7e/7ec27d6ca1e909c7e5a140acd67ae6e3a04425b8d2650c9a5c352f707a217e32.png)\n\nPassing one function as an input to another is known as **function composition**. This has a special notation,\n$(f \\circ g)(x)$,\nwhich simply means\n$f(g(x))$.\n\nThe key is to use the **inner function** $g(x)$ for the input into the **outer function** $f(x)$.\n\n**Watch out!** The composition of functions is *not* a product of functions. Note the subtle differences in notation here:\n\n- $f(g(x)) = (f\\circ g)(x)$ means \"take $g(x)$ and use it as the input into $f(x)$\".\n- $(fg)(x) = f(x)g(x)$ means \"take $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and multiply them together\".\n\nTo evaluate a composite function at a specific point, we evaluate the inner function at the specified value, and then we pass the result to the outer function." :tutorial/id #uuid "1fea0484-ec9d-58ad-ab60-d0a1f38110d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2752 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c9a2074-9e2a-5ae2-a75b-678963fe546e" :step/math-academy-id 458946 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128912 :question/id #uuid "10dabcd1-ab03-54e9-987f-97a1ea391b6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3915"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f954ff1-3f02-5fb1-aca2-5911d5636167" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522736}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Composite Function"} :step/id #uuid "b237dc04-f701-51e4-81d7-6bffa2123dff" :step/math-academy-id 458947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129077 :question/id #uuid "67de4022-de9d-5e39-a89a-c1ee70940198" :question/math-academy-id "e-9873"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a091472-6811-5b1f-ac4d-b0140b5d7123" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050566}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129555}] :knowledge-point/title "Function Composition With Tables"} :step/id #uuid "1862d0cb-e425-5641-9847-65c78357334b" :step/math-academy-id 458951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129300 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128913 :question/id #uuid "e0d6a3b4-1717-5ab7-8c61-fbce1b99d9af" :question/math-academy-id "e-3920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6376311d-698d-5c2d-9bfd-c96a944f82ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129556}] :knowledge-point/title "Composing a Function With Itself"} :step/id #uuid "e7e644e8-438d-51fe-94d2-9c0ba13792ec" :step/math-academy-id 458956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129301 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129076 :question/id #uuid "ea7d1bf0-5a2a-5d52-ad54-0f63ecfc5735" :question/math-academy-id "e-9870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c83590fb-144f-58a6-9bf5-b609525b3e47" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048217}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129494}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Function Composition"} :step/id #uuid "14e6f1de-50d3-559b-971f-4d5866098d20" :step/math-academy-id 458959}] :activity/title "Function Composition" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129333}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055528} :activity/id #uuid "14506510-6a23-50e7-9f92-2d6a0e7b9372" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782 :topic/id #uuid "66aee53c-aecf-5378-b318-bdc0930f5e43" :topic/math-academy-id 140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055964 :tutorial/content "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$\\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}$\n- **Subtraction**\n$(f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3$\n- **Multiplication**\n$(fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2$\n- **Division**\n$(f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "c656d962-19ad-5e04-b117-fa8d0c9034b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2751 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10d1d758-8351-5c00-a262-8b57b04f8398" :step/math-academy-id 13680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054522 :question/id #uuid "9a523530-ec38-5df8-997f-2f94975eb87b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516773}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "725779d7-8771-596d-841c-0ca2dd5da761" :step/math-academy-id 13682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054523 :question/id #uuid "79c8f0a9-6f05-515f-abaa-553863402a06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522813}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c6da4ec9-5a2e-5037-ad6d-2b91d02a4fa0" :step/math-academy-id 13683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054524 :question/id #uuid "c68e70d0-ccec-578a-96c1-7ed106aebc79" :question/math-academy-id "e-3914"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aafd8db-c723-508f-9ea3-43881f0d9140" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522719}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "722ef25c-0cfd-5916-9719-a70b3e79029b" :step/math-academy-id 13684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054544 :question/id #uuid "b6a4d0a8-f579-5d6d-ad74-59b68d2046e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410421}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "d9de7d87-ac26-5915-a421-2a3c6f706e14" :step/math-academy-id 15087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055971 :tutorial/content "When we're asked to evaluate the sum of two functions at a particular point, there are two methods that we can use. We could either evaluate both functions at the point and sum the results. Or, we could sum the two functions and then evaluate the result at the point.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the $(f+g)(1)$ for\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x-3\n$$\n\nThe notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f + g)(1)$, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$, and then add the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (1)^{2} - 1 = 0 \\\\\ng(1) &= 2(1) - 3 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= f(1) + g(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(x) &= (x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f + g)(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= (1)^{2} + 2(1) - 4 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth these methods work fine, but method $1$ is usually faster.\n\nFinally, note that we can evaluate the difference between two functions using the same methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "74671b66-6f9b-59a5-96e2-aeb7ca503afe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2834 :tutorial/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c0ba47b0-a276-56f7-965d-acc1c060fc97" :step/math-academy-id 15088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055551}}] :activity/title "The Arithmetic of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054889}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413867} :activity/id #uuid "034eac1a-9202-5b49-a5b1-6bfa6ac2f838" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782 :topic/id #uuid "66aee53c-aecf-5378-b318-bdc0930f5e43" :topic/math-academy-id 140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191ed9fb-e1ea-5bef-892a-5baa90256a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413837} {:answer/id #uuid "2d90ba2d-f17e-5fdb-9818-2e1002340d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413838} {:answer/id #uuid "325859ee-224e-52c1-a505-c5197b9a6558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413839} {:answer/id #uuid "65020d93-ef32-58e5-bf24-738b6719f093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413841} {:answer/id #uuid "e7666301-e17b-5314-afdb-d7835e5ea1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413841} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4da891c-9eb7-5195-a3f8-0ac1c8c6f04f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "23a164f0-a08a-5974-9621-a6231dc1af90" :question/math-academy-id "q-66137" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2x+1$ and $g(x)=-3x+5,$ find $(f+g)(4).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(4),$ we find $f(4)$ and $g(4),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(4)$ and $g(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2(4)+1=9 \\\\ g(4) & =-3(4)+5=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(4)=f(4)+g(4)=9+(-7)=2\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(2x+1)+(-3x+5) \\\\  & =-x+6 \\\\  & =6-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(4) & =6-4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03bc34b4-7201-59a8-8931-1f1c21fe49e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ace236d-44ca-52a6-8858-d6e6168991a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413836} {:answer/id #uuid "596e74cb-e10e-56ee-8a2b-0983ab1a5c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413840} {:answer/id #uuid "7494fadd-dd07-5f67-a41d-7917f368d4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413842} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f2e513-a3ca-5f51-9c1b-c18eeceadd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413853} {:answer/id #uuid "f632d377-ac04-57c7-af38-4a82afb34aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413856} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a311d46-6876-5cc8-bd29-ac82b7694535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fe5aa5dd-14e3-52ea-8c5e-90aad88d589b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78620" :question/problem "Find  $(f/g)(-1)$  for $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+x.$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then divide the results. \n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, since the denominator of a fraction can't be zero, we can't divide $f(-1)$ by $g(-1).$\n\nTherefore, the value  $(f/g)(-1)$  is undefined."} :step/id #uuid "292a564d-1238-5079-b27b-a11f9ef4f0f1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84f34919-47cb-54fa-a488-9daaa920449b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413844} {:answer/id #uuid "99c58b19-affa-5e56-ac31-b7ba0d67c4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413847} {:answer/id #uuid "d5863df3-ccee-5d78-a1f6-ffe664b5ed2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413850} {:answer/id #uuid "d88a8dac-6d79-5f3b-af46-b99926e48769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413851} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7784da-0b8b-5927-98d8-267858c58f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413850} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6c5b78a-506e-5248-bf6a-b9065365cf86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e96a222d-7bc2-5df0-a9b1-bdb0a30abe0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66422" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=4x-6$ and $g(x)=5x-10,$ find  $(fg)(4).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(4),$ we find $f(4)$ and $g(4),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(4)$ and $g(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4(4)-6=10 \\\\ g(4) & =5(4)-10=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(4)=f(4)g(4)=10\\cdot 10=100\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(4x-6)(5x-10) \\\\  & =20{x}^{2}-70x+60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(4) & =20(4)^{2}-70(4)+60 \\\\  & =320-280+60 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e2f3d8e-766a-5816-a6bd-fad48a7d5c1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a971ff-a0ac-5e41-a6f9-de3fae7b9090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413834} {:answer/id #uuid "79f9695d-1add-50ce-be55-ea88b6f4de22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413843} {:answer/id #uuid "970de561-023f-5e4a-8ecd-0cd2dba960b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413846} {:answer/id #uuid "e137be1f-e344-5693-8401-73a72931c8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413852} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2ca1be-c620-54b9-9e8c-878f37709564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413846} :answer-field/id #uuid "328f71c0-868a-51e6-9077-92f3e52307b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d54abce-95cd-5dd9-be80-9f173369bea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66425" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(3).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2(3)^{2}-6=12 \\\\ g(3) & =-2(3)+2=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(3)=\\frac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{-2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(3) & =\\frac{2(3)^{2}-6}{-2(3)+2}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4e39718-e05a-5c8f-b46d-8cb5708b44e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0624c52e-1d19-5b3b-ba86-d2f6b9cc8ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413835} {:answer/id #uuid "881fa5d4-1c12-5bc5-a457-efc8e41af6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413845} {:answer/id #uuid "9a61e467-6a0c-55c5-bb23-f1d334a8e5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413848} {:answer/id #uuid "c363b46a-0dfe-5b02-aa34-a0ac7fb02ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413849} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d08458-0ba1-59ee-877c-a0b12c255dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413835} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb5f2ce0-c095-5ce3-be9e-db12b1e6fb29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8d2e394-0754-5534-a3fd-34ae3db4d32c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66348" :question/problem "Given $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $g(x)=2x-1,$ find  $(fg)(3).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =(3)^{2}+1=10 \\\\ g(3) & =2(3)-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(3)=f(3)g(3)=10\\cdot 5=50\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)(2x-1) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}+2(3)-1 \\\\  & =54-9+6-1 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc41b5fb-c1e3-5c52-a028-1ec2c66a6df0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413869}}] :activity/title "The Arithmetic of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413833}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516973} :activity/id #uuid "7ce49917-85e7-5228-8878-60a8c1f29960" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782 :topic/id #uuid "66aee53c-aecf-5378-b318-bdc0930f5e43" :topic/math-academy-id 140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cc163ae-67c3-5dbb-9c2e-df65985cece8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516268} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4f1670-871c-59ee-8a82-19f3fd41d372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516308} {:answer/id #uuid "7d98d607-1295-57e0-82a2-4957c73eb6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516378} {:answer/id #uuid "b56a0556-d0b6-58d1-ae92-072bdafe84ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516490} {:answer/id #uuid "c9da03d1-95db-5bd2-ac01-db50a9b70c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516308} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff0d330b-f0bf-5ed3-9055-16241719d8a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0dffdb23-4e19-5e3b-9c5d-b76e7cdb23fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-66419" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=8{x}^{2}+1$ and $g(x)=2x-2,$ find  $(fg)(1).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(1),$ we find $f(1)$ and $g(1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =8(1)^{2}+1=9 \\\\ g(1) & =2(1)-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(1)=f(1)g(1)=9\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(8{x}^{2}+1)(2x-2) \\\\  & =16{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(1) & =16(1)^{3}-16(1)^{2}+2(1)-2 \\\\  & =16-16+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8915ea14-a53d-5e03-ae86-339fcc7361b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a971ff-a0ac-5e41-a6f9-de3fae7b9090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413834} {:answer/id #uuid "79f9695d-1add-50ce-be55-ea88b6f4de22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413843} {:answer/id #uuid "970de561-023f-5e4a-8ecd-0cd2dba960b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413846} {:answer/id #uuid "e137be1f-e344-5693-8401-73a72931c8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413852} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2ca1be-c620-54b9-9e8c-878f37709564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413846} :answer-field/id #uuid "328f71c0-868a-51e6-9077-92f3e52307b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d54abce-95cd-5dd9-be80-9f173369bea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66425" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(3).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2(3)^{2}-6=12 \\\\ g(3) & =-2(3)+2=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(3)=\\frac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{-2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(3) & =\\frac{2(3)^{2}-6}{-2(3)+2}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c74026d0-f786-50c1-b154-b7d927f144aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232f6d1-cc47-55de-8533-bead91016a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516117} {:answer/id #uuid "3581fa24-c005-5f7c-a7ef-a44af78898c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516225} {:answer/id #uuid "574a7f7b-4e6e-5629-b9e0-03d3d14a8260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516295} {:answer/id #uuid "ae50b601-00f2-5d2d-862d-a236109f9bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516475} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4ed9aa-c01d-5794-9c67-4387dd7bdb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516475} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b5e342-2f59-5f17-97d7-60225f6e5208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98638830-1bd5-5922-b12a-9c190db0e3da" :question/math-academy-id "q-4825" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x$ and $g(x)=9-2x,$ find $(fg)(-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}+5(-1)=-3 \\\\ g(-1) & =9-2(-1)=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=-3\\cdot 11=-33\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+5x)(9-2x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+45x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =-4(-1)^{3}+8(-1)^{2}+45(-1) \\\\  & =4+8-45 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dbee05de-3d07-576a-9368-57acc32e5170" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02da732e-8f8f-522b-a0af-c292550ebd0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516118} {:answer/id #uuid "12fd6f4f-4886-505e-ae42-dc7cc6984c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516148} {:answer/id #uuid "2045e6fa-2eac-591c-966c-1fdb56bcfe80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516173} {:answer/id #uuid "5f02d874-962f-54b5-b78b-81b45af705f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516318} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaec706-9934-5e79-9ce3-c47dee2c41e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516173} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0def8c5-0e76-5633-984d-4a0398a720ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76e8734a-ae7e-5bfd-82cf-840027d7b221" :question/math-academy-id "q-66437" :question/problem "Given $f(x)={x}^{2}-4$ and $g(x)=2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(2).$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =(2)^{2}-4=0 \\\\ g(2) & =2(2)+2=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(2)=\\frac{f(2)}{g(2)}=\\frac{0}{6}=0\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(2):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(2) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}-4}{2(2)+2}=\\frac{0}{6}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f577bcab-36ae-5324-94f9-2ea6a15c229b"}] :activity/title "The Arithmetic of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516103}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084318} :activity/id #uuid "66462250-9bf6-5b79-837b-72d46f60efbf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867 :topic/id #uuid "6cb8c050-5f50-5c96-a3dc-144c8d409165" :topic/math-academy-id 753} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084499 :tutorial/content "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f.$ The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f,$** and denote by $f^{-1}.$\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f.$ For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2),$ we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}.\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f,$ that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that $f({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2},$  and as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0.$\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "05fd6236-8b7b-59d7-bf60-5e7d9d2db65a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f836d465-3cfd-57b6-ab05-79619fabc9a6" :step/math-academy-id 41715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082904 :question/id #uuid "bca42c78-0735-587e-914b-7c9892f47d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-10986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521506}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "2e3f4334-5c00-58e4-9032-fed944bab672" :step/math-academy-id 41716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082905 :question/id #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521521}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"} :step/id #uuid "d19f2942-df0f-5896-adf0-8c5b3ca192b5" :step/math-academy-id 41719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082906 :question/id #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 